      heading: "Brand-name, compounded, or pills: what is legal and what you are actually buying",
      body: `<p>This is the section to read twice, because it is where most of the money and most of the risk sits. "GLP-1 online" covers four different products that are sold with similar language and very different legal standing.</p>
<h3>Brand-name pens and pills: FDA-approved, priced accordingly</h3>
<p>Wegovy was approved for chronic weight management on June 4, 2021 (<a href="https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-new-drug-treatment-chronic-weight-management-first-2014" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FDA press announcement</a>) and Zepbound on November 8, 2023 (<a href="https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-new-medication-chronic-weight-management" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FDA press announcement</a>). Each dose is a factory-made, sealed pen whose contents were tested in the trials above and whose manufacturing is inspected by the FDA. The Wegovy pill and Foundayo carry the same status. The cash prices online reflect that: on the providers we have verified, brand-name Zepbound is $1,249 a month at altRx and $1,599 at welludr; Ozempic is $1,399 at welludr; Ro sells Zepbound KwikPens from $299 the first month then $399-$449, and the Wegovy pill at $149 the first month then $299, in each case plus its membership. Our <a href="/weight-loss/articles/zepbound-price-online">Zepbound price guide</a> and <a href="/weight-loss/articles/wegovy-pill-price-online">Wegovy pill price guide</a> keep these current and explain the manufacturers' own direct-pay programs, whose prices we report from dated third-party sources but have not verified ourselves.</p>
<h3>Compounded semaglutide and tirzepatide: legal under conditions, not FDA-approved</h3>
<p>Compounding is the practice of a licensed pharmacist preparing a medication for an individual patient under a prescription. US federal law allows it under two frameworks, both described on the FDA's <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding" rel="noopener" target="_blank">human drug compounding</a> page. <strong>Section 503A</strong> covers state-licensed pharmacies compounding for a specific patient on a specific prescription; this is the route almost every telehealth GLP-1 program uses, which is why "503A pharmacy" appears so often on provider sites. <strong>Section 503B</strong> covers registered outsourcing facilities that can produce larger batches under tighter federal oversight.</p>
<p>The reason compounded GLP-1s exploded in 2023 and 2024 is that both semaglutide and tirzepatide were on the FDA's drug shortage list, and the law allows compounding of a drug in shortage with fewer restrictions. That window has closed. The FDA declared the tirzepatide shortage resolved on December 19, 2024, and the semaglutide shortage resolved on February 21, 2025, with enforcement grace periods that ended in the spring of 2025 (<a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-clarifies-policies-compounders-national-glp-1-supply-begins-stabilize" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FDA statement on compounder policies</a>). What remains legal is what was always legal: a 503A pharmacy may compound for an individual patient when a prescriber documents a clinical need that the approved product does not meet - a different dose, a different formulation, an added ingredient, an inability to tolerate an excipient. Mass-producing copies of the approved drug is not permitted. Enforcement is ongoing and the rules continue to evolve; the practical consequence for you is that the prescription should be patient-specific and the pharmacy should be named.</p>
<p>The FDA's own summary of the risks is worth reading in full: <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/postmarket-drug-safety-information-patients-and-providers/fdas-concerns-unapproved-glp-1-drugs-used-weight-loss" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FDA's Concerns with Unapproved GLP-1 Drugs Used for Weight Loss</a>. The agency's points, in short: compounded products are not reviewed for safety, effectiveness or quality before they are sold; it has received reports of adverse events including dosing errors from vials that require the patient to measure a dose; some products have been found to contain salt forms of semaglutide (semaglutide sodium, semaglutide acetate) that are not the active ingredient in the approved drugs; and products sold without a prescription or from unlicensed sources may be counterfeit.</p>
<p>None of that means compounded GLP-1 is a scam. It means the quality lives in the pharmacy, not in a brand, and that you are trusting the telehealth provider's choice of pharmacy. The providers on this site name theirs, and that is the first thing we check. Our guide to <a href="/weight-loss/articles/compounded-semaglutide-vs-brand-name">compounded vs brand-name semaglutide</a> and the <a href="/weight-loss/articles/503a-vs-503b-pharmacy-28-day-rule">503A vs 503B explainer</a> go deeper, including the "28-day rule" on beyond-use dating that determines how much medication a pharmacy can ship at once.</p>
<h3>Compounded "pills," "drops" and "gum": a different thing again</h3>
<p>Some programs sell compounded oral semaglutide as tablets, sublingual drops or troches. These are not the FDA-approved Wegovy pill, which is a specific formulation designed to survive the stomach; they are compounded preparations with no trial data of their own, and oral absorption of a peptide is poor without the formulation work the approved pill went through. If you want a pill, the two approved options exist. If a program's "pill" is compounded, understand that you are buying an unstudied product, not a cheaper Wegovy pill. Our <a href="/weight-loss/weight-loss-pills">weight-loss pills guide</a> separates the categories.</p>
<h3>The one-sentence test</h3>
<p>If a product is described as "FDA-approved" and it is compounded, the description is false - the FDA said so in a June 2026 warning letter to altRx's parent company over marketing that suggested approval, as we note in our <a href="/weight-loss/reviews/altrx">altRx review</a>. If a product requires no prescription, it is not a legal GLP-1 in the United States. Everything else is a question of price, pharmacy and terms, which the rest of this guide is about.</p>`,
